  nodeTextLayers:
  - title: 'What is an Insulin Monomer?



'
    basic: "An insulin monomer is a small protein produced by the pancreatic beta
      cell. \nThe monomer form of insulin is its active form which can interact with
      receptors on other cells"
    intermediate: 
    advanced: "Insulin is synthesized as preproinsulin and subsequently processed
      into proinsulin in the Golgi apparatus. \nProinsulin is then sequestered into
      insulin vesicles and processed into insulin. \nInsulin is composed of two polypeptide
      chains: the 21 residue A-chain and the 30 residue B-chain. Two disulfide bonds
      connect the A and B chain. A third disulfide bond in chain A makes an intra-chain
      linkage. \nThese structural features of insulin are highly conserved among
      mammals."
  nodeSpriteLayer: []
